WEBVTT

00:00:09,360 --> 00:00:13,680
What does it mean when my AST is elevated?

00:00:13,680 --> 00:00:20,480
So AST is an enzyme, it's in the comprehensive metabolic profile.

00:00:20,480 --> 00:00:21,440
That's what you're going to see it.

00:00:21,440 --> 00:00:27,200
Or maybe times people will just get a liver panel done at their medical doctor.

00:00:28,480 --> 00:00:34,880
And AST, ALT and alkaline phosphatase are considered the liver enzymes.

00:00:34,880 --> 00:00:39,120
But in truth, those enzymes are in other tissues too.

00:00:39,120 --> 00:00:45,760
So AST is going to be produced in more highly metabolic tissues.

00:00:46,720 --> 00:00:53,680
Your muscles make AST. Your liver, obviously, your heart, your kidneys, your lungs,

00:00:55,200 --> 00:01:03,040
when you have low AST, typically that's a vitamin B6 deficiency.

00:01:03,040 --> 00:01:04,960
So we don't see that very much.

00:01:05,040 --> 00:01:11,120
So most people, you know, you don't have to be taking vitamin B complex or something like that

00:01:11,120 --> 00:01:15,680
to be getting your B's. If you're eating vegetables, even good meat, you're still getting

00:01:15,680 --> 00:01:21,040
your B vitamins. And you're not going to end up with some gross vitamin B6 deficiency.

00:01:21,040 --> 00:01:27,600
But it does happen. Some people have really bad gut absorption sites for your B vitamins.

00:01:27,600 --> 00:01:32,480
Some people's diet has been really poor. And they can't end up with vitamin B6 deficiency.

00:01:32,480 --> 00:01:37,040
It can be a vitamin B6idemia is a real thing. It's just not real common.

00:01:37,680 --> 00:01:43,280
Most of the time, you're looking at elevations of AST. That's what this question is.

00:01:43,280 --> 00:01:51,760
So obviously, the first thing you think of is liver. So when AST and ALT are elevated together,

00:01:51,760 --> 00:01:56,000
especially with alkaline phosphatase, the first thing you're going to think of is liver stress.

00:01:56,880 --> 00:02:02,800
But in truth, it's not always the liver. You could have AST elevated with early congestive

00:02:02,800 --> 00:02:09,760
heart failure. So again, remember, any blood test is a clue. And it's a clue that should get your

00:02:09,760 --> 00:02:15,360
doctor to ask other questions. You know, and I'm trying to get you guys to think like a doctor here.

00:02:15,360 --> 00:02:22,000
So I have elevated AST. What does that mean? Could be my liver has got issues. So there's other things

00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:28,880
you'd look at too in conjunction with that. Again, like ALT and alkaline phosphatase also elevated,

00:02:28,880 --> 00:02:40,160
well, that it's more probable that it's my liver. If my GGT, which is another test, is elevated,

00:02:40,160 --> 00:02:48,560
then I would think that it's maybe outside of the liver. So again, AST can be elevated with

00:02:48,640 --> 00:02:54,560
congestive heart failure. But then you're going to look at and decrease CO2 level. So CO2 is

00:02:54,560 --> 00:03:04,240
another test that you would see in a comprehensive metabolic panel. And then you'd also look at LDH,

00:03:04,240 --> 00:03:09,600
which is a typically the comprehensive metabolic panel. But LDH is another thing if your person

00:03:09,600 --> 00:03:20,000
has congestive heart failure, LDH can be elevated as well. Same thing with cardiovascular issues.

00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:26,080
If you have cardiovascular issues, AST can be increased. ALT can be increased with cardiovascular

00:03:26,080 --> 00:03:35,280
issues too, but you'd have an increase in CO2. And typically an increase in uric acid with cardiovascular

00:03:35,280 --> 00:03:45,680
issues. But most commonly people are going to think of liver dysfunction with AST. Plus it depends

00:03:45,680 --> 00:03:55,040
on what your current health issues are. So if I know I have cancer or let's say I know I'm taking a

00:03:55,040 --> 00:04:04,400
medication. So again, one of the most common causes of liver congestion, liver issues, liver

00:04:04,400 --> 00:04:11,440
dysfunction is a medication that you're taking. So if I'm taking a medication, even like a

00:04:11,440 --> 00:04:21,040
fenmedazole or an Ivermectin or Tylenol. So Tylenol, remember, statistically in America there's

00:04:21,040 --> 00:04:30,560
15,000 people a year on average that die of Tylenol toxicity. What is it? What's Tylenol toxicity?

00:04:30,560 --> 00:04:34,880
Have you ever heard of that? Well, of course you don't hear of that because they're listed in

00:04:34,880 --> 00:04:40,400
their autopsies and die in a liver failure because it causes liver dysfunction. Their AST, LDH,

00:04:40,400 --> 00:04:45,600
it's possible basically through the roof, the liver is shut down and the person died. So that nobody

00:04:45,600 --> 00:04:51,120
ever said that it's from Tylenol, but you can look up at the annuals and it is from toxicity,

00:04:51,120 --> 00:04:59,440
from Tylenol, and over the counter medication. So medications are the number one thing that causes

00:04:59,440 --> 00:05:06,160
liver issues because they're, you're taking them in quantities that it's very difficult

00:05:06,160 --> 00:05:14,720
to detoxify. So it has been measured that different medications detoxify on different pathways

00:05:14,720 --> 00:05:20,240
in the liver. If you already have, you know, issues with your NAT1 pathways, which a lot of

00:05:20,240 --> 00:05:27,280
meds detoxify other different cytochrome P450 pathways, you have genetic defects on those pathways.

00:05:27,280 --> 00:05:32,720
And then you're taking these meds, it's going to be difficult to detoxify them and it can't

00:05:32,720 --> 00:05:40,320
overwhelm those pathways and it can damage the liver cells. And that's what liver failure is.

00:05:40,320 --> 00:05:45,920
So you will see a spike in AST and ALT and typically alkaline phosphatase if that is the case.

00:05:46,560 --> 00:05:50,880
Problem is, if you're not doing blood tests on a regular basis and you're on these medications,

00:05:50,880 --> 00:05:55,040
well, how do you, how are you going to know? That's why I ask if you're going to be on

00:05:55,040 --> 00:06:00,240
a medisol that you're doing a regular comprehensive metabolic panel so we can keep an eye on things so

00:06:00,240 --> 00:06:06,000
we're not seeing any spike. Plus, you could have an allergic reaction to a medication that can cause

00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:11,280
liver failure. So we get more complicated. So you want to keep an eye on these. You don't do these

00:06:11,280 --> 00:06:16,400
blood tests just for no reason. You do these blood tests so we can see if there's damage,

00:06:16,960 --> 00:06:23,200
if the liver is being compromised in any way. So those are the things that you look at with AST

00:06:23,200 --> 00:06:28,480
elevated. You compare them with like any blood test, any specific individual marker, you compare it

00:06:28,480 --> 00:06:33,760
with other markers to get a differential diagnosis and then you use other tests as well.

00:06:34,560 --> 00:06:40,640
Based upon the clues that you get from your blood work to give you data to dig deeper to figure out

00:06:40,640 --> 00:06:46,240
the reason why you're always looking for the reason why. And to me, it's a very shameful thing when I see

00:06:46,320 --> 00:06:56,400
people's blood work and it was ordered by their doctor and the doctor never went over it. So it's

00:06:57,840 --> 00:07:01,360
why is your order then? I don't get it. So that's another

